{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011Cg2hRNunuCFS9HxZngdag","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full picture. Let me analyze what needs to be fixed:\n\n1. **graph/graph.go** - `Order()` needs:\n   - Deterministic topological order (sorted)\n   - Detect cycles (self-cycles and multi-node cycles)\n   - Error for missing dependencies\n   \n2. **parse/parse.go** - `Load()` needs:\n   - Reject duplicate names\n   - Reject empty names\n   - Reject missing dependencies (or this could be in graph)\n   \n3. **plan/plan.go** - `Build()` should work once graph and parse are fixed\n4. **cache/cache.go** needs to key by actual input data rather than just last-set status, and should never mask errors -- cache valid results only, always re-run on failure.\n5. **batch/batch.go** needs to fail atomically, returning nil on any error and surfacing the first error encountered.\n6. **cmd/planner/main.go** needs newline-terminated output and a nonzero exit code on error. Now I should trace through the test requirements, starting with the graph tests.\n\nThe test expects `Order()` to return a deterministic, sorted topological ordering across many repeated runs with the same input, and to return an error when there's a self-cycle in the dependency graph.\n\nFor `Build()`, I'm testing that a valid dependency chain resolves to a correct comma-separated plan string, then checking several failure cases: missing dependencies, circular dependencies, duplicate node names, and empty names all should produce errors.\n\nI'm also writing a cache invalidation test confirming that building different inputs returns different results, that a broken input errors out correctly without being masked by caching, and that rebuilding the same valid input afterward still works.\n\nNow I'm thinking through a batch test: when all inputs succeed, it should return all results in order; when one input fails, the entire batch should return nil output with an error.\n\nI realize the cache is keyed per input, so repeated identical entries get cached hits while distinct ones compute fresh — that matches the expected behavior.\n\nNext I'm setting up a CLI test that runs the planner binary and feeds it stdin input to check end-to-end behavior.\n\nFor the CLI tests, I need the output to have a trailing newline for the success case, and a nonzero exit code when a dependency cycle is detected.\n\nNow I'm turning to the graph package: I need to implement a proper topological sort with cycle detection, and make sure the output ordering is deterministic (alphabetically sorted among valid orderings) to match what the tests expect.\n\nChecking a case where nodes have no dependencies confirms they should just come out in alphabetical order, and chaining dependencies like app→lib→base should resolve to base, lib, app. I'm also considering whether the missing-dependency check belongs in the graph logic or in the parsing step, since the parse test loads nodes referencing a dependency that doesn't exist, and I need to decide which layer should catch and report that error.\n\nNow I'm sketching the module design: parse.Load should reject empty names and duplicates, graph.Order should catch missing dependencies and cycles while producing a deterministic topological order, cache.go should key by the input data itself and avoid caching errors, batch.go should short-circuit on the first error, and main.go needs to handle output formatting.\n\nFor the topological sort, I'm implementing Kahn's algorithm, building an in-degree map for all nodes and checking each dependency reference against the graph to catch missing deps.\n\nI need to double check the direction of the edges here: if \"lib depends on base,\" that means base must come before lib, so base points to lib, meaning in-degree(lib) counts how many dependencies lib still has unprocessed.\n\nSo I'll set up the algorithm with in-degree equal to the number of dependencies each node has, plus a reverse map tracking which nodes depend on each node, so I can decrement in-degrees as nodes get processed. First I'm validating that all dependencies actually exist in the graph before building these structures.\n\nI notice appending the sorted batch to the queue doesn't actually keep the whole queue sorted overall—I need to maintain proper ordering by re-sorting after each insertion, or use a sorted insert approach instead.\n\nRewriting Kahn's algorithm with deterministic tie-breaking...\n\nFinishing the deletion step and decrementing dependent in-degrees after each pick, giving an O(n²) but deterministic algorithm. I trace through the example cases to verify it correctly handles ties alphabetically and resolves chains like app→lib→base in the right order.\n\nI also confirm it properly detects self-cycles and two-node cycles by finding no candidates and returning an error in both situations.\n\nNow I'm setting up a simple cache keyed by the raw input bytes converted to a string, initializing the map lazily if needed.\n\nChecking against the test case confirms the logic works correctly: distinct keys get computed and cached separately, errors from broken input aren't cached, and repeated calls with the same key return the cached result.\n\nSince `last` is unexported, I can safely swap it for a map field without breaking the public API, since the zero-value `Cache{}` still needs to work and the map just needs lazy initialization on first use. For the batch function, I need to verify that when one input in a batch fails, the whole call returns nil and an error rather than partial results.\n\nI'm also confirming that each call to Build creates a fresh cache instance, so repeated identical inputs within the same batch still hit the cache correctly, like the third entry matching the first.\n\nFor the CLI, I need to make sure errors cause a proper exit code rather than falling through to a zero exit. In the parser, I'll add validation for empty names and duplicate keys, returning formatted errors when those conditions occur. Now I'm ready to write out all the files with these fixes.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":515,"cache_read_input_tokens":18285,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":515},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be85a4b6-3e88-4742-b354-1cb87807fd8c","uuid":"ac79154f-f17e-425b-a077-343c04039cbd"}
